WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:05.421
From acclaimed indie dramas to massive Marvel
blockbusters, Mark Ruffalo has proven he can

00:00:05.421 --> 00:00:05.980
do it all.

00:00:06.421 --> 00:00:09.680
But that doesn't mean that he nails every
scene on the first take — and we've got

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:11.880
just the collection of hilarious bloopers
to prove it.

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:17.080
After a couple of false starts with Eric Bana
and Edward Norton, Marvel finally found a

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.610
credible Hulk in Mark Ruffalo.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.800
While he has yet to get a standalone movie,
he's been a big green part of the Avengers

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.060
films and has popped up in a number of Marvel
Cinematic Universe entries.

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:32.260
While Hulk is primarily the work of CGI, Ruffalo
does the heavy lifting as the Dr. Jekyll to

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.280
Hulk's Mr. Hyde, as gifted scientist Bruce
Banner.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.880
"Your work in anti electronic collisions is
unparalleled.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:42.440
And I'm a huge fan of the way you lose control
and turn into an enormous green rage monster.”

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:46.500
Maybe it's because we "wouldn't like him when
he's angry," or maybe it's because the Hulk

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:50.510
and Banner are two different entities at the
end of the day — but whatever the reason,

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:54.070
Ruffalo decided that Hulk wasn't going to
stick around to Hulk-smash this particular

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:55.100
Avengers scene…

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.100
“"Dr. Banner.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:57.600
Now might be a really good time for you to
get angry."

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.570
"That's my secret Captain.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.050
I'm always angry."

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:05.000
This version, on the other hand, didn't make
the cut:

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:08.720
"Duuudes, you're on your own!”

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:16.040
Like the rest of his fellow Avengers — and
the majority of the actors who've taken on

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:20.400
major roles in the MCU — Ruffalo has to
cope with a certain amount of green screen

00:01:20.409 --> 00:01:24.920
acting, up to and including the hours he's
spent on set playing the Hulk via a motion-capture

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:29.240
rig that acts as the basis for the CGI-driven
depiction of the big green guy that we see

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:29.800
onscreen.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.960
But Ruffalo has it a little easier than most,
given that he doesn't often have to speak

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:38.170
much while playing the Hulk, but that definitely
isn't to say he hasn't faced some of his own

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:38.960
dialogue challenges.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:43.850
These Avengers movies are, at their core,
science-fiction flicks, and he's playing the

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:45.800
most prominent scientist around, in them.

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:51.040
That means it's up to Ruffalo to deliver lots
of technical jargon and scientific word gumbo.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:54.800
And, even an actor of Ruffalo's caliber has
a tough time with it, sometimes.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:58.540
"Per-potent cells inside a xylo…

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.120
Per-potent cells into a xylon cycl…

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:04.940
So was the third time the charm?

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.880
"Polysaccharide nano fiber…”

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:12.600
The Avengers movies offer an embarrassment
of riches when it comes to action stars.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:17.280
So much of their huge casts consist of established
veterans within the genre, serving up the

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.280
physical skills necessary to bring their superhero
characters to life.

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:24.240
Scarlett Johansson, Chris Hemsworth, Jeremy
Renner, and Chris Evans, among others, have

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:29.360
all jumped, kicked, and punched their way
through other movies… but not so much Ruffalo.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:34.060
Versed in theater, he's more of a "words"
and "face" actor, which are vital when it

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:37.900
comes to playing Bruce Banner, the human,
pre-anger iteration of the monstrous Hulk.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:42.080
That said, he seemed to have a hard time with
the minor physical demands of his role in

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:42.620
Thor: Ragnarok.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:45.880
At least Thor is there to rescue him.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:51.100
Despite his many onscreen appearances as a
superhero, and starring in three enjoyably

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:55.110
ridiculous movies about magicians — Now
You See Me, The Brothers Bloom, and Now You

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:58.460
See Me 2 — Ruffalo is widely regarded as
a serious actor.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:02.900
That's probably because his powerful work
in affecting, award-winning dramas like The

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:07.420
Normal Heart and Spotlight overshadow the
more frivolous, if financially successful,

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:08.600
line items on his resume.

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:13.060
"Mark, what's he get out of all of this?”

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:18.880
He maintains a quiet dignity and self-respect
through all his work, so when a script requires

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.400
him to say something kind of stupid, it's
a real treat — both to hear Ruffalo get

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.520
silly, and to see how he pulls it off without
looking bad.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:31.080
Well, it turns out, he can't quite pull it
off during a take on 13 Going on 30.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:31.740
This was his big moment:

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:35.160
"You know what I wish I had right now?”

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:36.260
“No what?"

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:38.200
"Razzles.”

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:40.680
“Razzles?”

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:44.060
And this was his epic fail:

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.180
“"I could really use a razzle.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:48.740
I really need a razzle…”

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.700
Ruffalo wasn't always an A-list superhero,
and Reese Witherspoon wasn't always a frequently

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:01.720
Oscar-nominated actress and behind-the-scenes
boss of stuff like Big Little Lies.

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:06.920
In 2005, Ruffalo and Witherspoon were both
making a name for themselves with romantic

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.700
comedies, particularly high-concept ones like
Just Like Heaven.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:15.460
Witherspoon plays a doctor in a coma whose
spirit leaves her body to go haunt her apartment,

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:18.940
where an affable landscape architect, played
by Ruffalo, has taken residence.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:21.040
"We need to talk."

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:21.880
"About what?"

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:27.020
"Has it crossed your mind that there might
be something a little off about the way you've

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:29.080
been spending your days?"

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:34.040
Eventually, Witherspoon gets better and they
magically live happily ever after.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:37.460
But in this outtake, Ruffalo comes on a little
too strong with his leading lady.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.080
Here was his best take:

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:42.780
"Has anything uh dramatic happened to you
recently?”

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:43.320
“Like what?”

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:45.740
“Like dying maybe?"

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:47.120
"How dare you say that to me?”

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:49.320
And here was his worst:

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:53.380
"Do you remember anything dramatic happening
to you recently.”

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:53.940
“Like what?”

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:57.120
“I dunno, like dying maybe?"

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:03.000
While filming another scene from the movie,
Ruffalo seemingly couldn't resist delivering

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:07.870
this improvised quip to his scantily clad
scene partner, when his very-mid-2000s cell

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:09.120
phone started ringing.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:12.380
“Is that your phone ringing?”

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:15.600
“No I'm just happy to see you.”

